Source package: gnome-session

leave-scope-to-glib.patch
    Lets gnome-session leave the scope of an application it starts at login to GLib, when the
    platform data of GLib's "launched" signal names one as "systemd-unit". The GLib of
    patches/glib2.0/ puts the application into that scope before it starts, and a request of
    gnome-session for the scope afterwards would only fail, with a warning in the journal. Drop the
    patch together with patches/glib2.0/scope-before-exec.patch.

    The request is made in app_launched in gnome-session/gsm-util.c. The patch adds no test, as
    gnome-session's tests do not launch applications.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login:

      journalctl --user -b | grep 'Could not create transient scope'
                                  should find nothing
